WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.327
[MUSIC]

00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:05.649
Let's talk about the sort
of perception of time.

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:08.800
Now, what the research shows us is that
perception of experienced time is largely

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.220
different by attention.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.750
Time bothers us when we
pay attention to it.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.339
And so the name of the game is
to give people distractions,

00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:20.424
it makes time feel like
it went by quicker, okay?

00:00:20.424 --> 00:00:24.020
So give your customers
something to do while waiting.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.940
Compare an elevator ride.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.730
Some companies use music, but
usually, they use irritating music.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:34.880
But what if you play them the news or
some kind of interesting podcast?

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:40.470
And then it almost becomes something,
it's distracting and fun to be waiting.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.910
The other thing to do to manage time
is give people a sense of progress.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:48.170
Okay, the one thing we really
don't like is not knowing, right?

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.430
Not knowing when this
thing is going to end.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.320
Actually, airlines used to be bad at this.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.290
You had a boarding time, but it was
never kind of clear exactly when it was.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.660
And now they've shifted to telling you
exactly when the boarding time is.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.420
And they even have
a countdown telling you,

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.600
boarding is going to commence
in X amount of minutes.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.920
The exact same amount of time where
you know kind of you have a sense of

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.190
progress versus you don't have a sense
of progress feels completely different.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:18.670
And so Disney recognizes that it has
people waiting in lines for a ton of time.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:23.140
And so what they do is they have these
kind of mileposts where they say,

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:28.690
this is how much time you have left to go,
to give you a sense of what's happening.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.810
Because what we really don't like, because
we don't like to have an experience where

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.830
we don't have control, right?

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.080
So you want to give people
a sense of control.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.510
What matters is not actual control, but
it's your sense of perceived control.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.760
Do I feel like I'm controlling
the time that's passing?

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.680
Or is this something else controlling
it and I have no control over it?

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.700
Most of the time, customers
are engaging in these experiences and

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.960
they're not cynical.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.420
When I go to Disney,
I know they're messing with my mind

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.309
with all the information about
the waiting time, but it still works.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.070
You can have an objective time in
which something happened that's

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:14.110
very different than the subjective
time in which something happened.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:19.820
So the question is, how do you manage
that subjective sense of elapsed time?

